      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The word "epoxy" on a label tells you the family of chemistry. It tells you nothing about how much of that chemistry ends up on your floor. That number is the solids content, and it is the single most useful thing on the can.
        </p>

        <h2 id="solids-and-thickness" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Solids Content and Film Thickness
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every liquid coating is made of two things: the material that will become the cured film, and the carrier that makes it thin enough to spread. The carrier evaporates. The solids stay. When a can says "48 per cent solids by volume", it is telling you that roughly half of what you roll onto the floor will leave as vapour over the next day, and the film you are left with is about half as thick as the wet coat you applied.
        </p>

        <p>
          Water-based garage epoxies, which is what the majority of big-box kits are, generally run 40 to 60 per cent solids. Applied at the recommended spread rate, the wet coat is around 5 to 7 mils and the dry film ends up at 2 to 4 mils. For reference, a sheet of printer paper is about 4 mils thick. Two coats of a water-based epoxy gives you a total film comparable to one or two sheets of paper. It is a real coating and it does a real job, but it is thin.
        </p>

        <p>
          Solvent-based epoxies sit in the middle, typically 50 to 70 per cent solids. They were the standard DIY product twenty years ago and have been squeezed out by VOC regulations in many states. Where you can still buy them they lay down 4 to 7 mils dry per coat and have excellent penetration into the concrete, but the solvent odour is severe.
        </p>

        <p>
          A 100 per cent solids epoxy has no carrier at all. What you mix is what you get. The two components react with each other to form the film and nothing evaporates, so a wet coat of 10 mils cures to a dry coat of 10 mils. Typical garage applications run 8 to 20 mils per coat depending on how aggressively it is spread, and some self-levelling formulas are poured at 30 mils or more. A single coat of 100 per cent solids is three to five times thicker than a coat of water-based, and that thickness is where almost every performance advantage comes from.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Type</th>
                <th>Solids by Volume</th>
                <th>Dry Film per Coat</th>
                <th>Typical Use</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Water-based epoxy</td>
                <td>40 to 60 per cent</td>
