        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Solids content is film thickness, and film thickness is lifespan. A water-based epoxy is a thin, easy, low-odour coating that gives a few years of clean floor for a couple of hundred dollars. A 100 per cent solids epoxy is a thick, demanding, unforgiving coating that gives a decade or two for four times the money and a genuinely hard weekend.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            Neither is the wrong answer. The wrong answer is buying the thin one expecting the thick one's performance, or buying the thick one without a helper, a plan and a cool morning to apply it. Decide what you want the floor to do, then read the solids number, not the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

